= 23 September 1987

Yugoslavia faces severe challenges. But much as Metternich said that, when Paris sneezes, Europe catches a cold - so too, when Serbia sneezes, Yugoslavia catches lung cancer. And currently, Serbia is tearing herself apart at the seams. A bright new man, pupil of the current President of Serbia - Ivan Stambolić - has taken to the work of field politics, and come out of it a hero. Slobodan Milošević, a banker, English speaker, and most ruthless of pragmatists, has boxed his own mentor in. And he is shooting for power.

On April 24th, Milošević was sent to Kosovo as the Serbian branch of the League of Communists' representative, meant to quell and ease the nationalist fury of the Serbs in Kosovo. The Serbs, ever since Ranković, feeling under attack or otherwise dispossessed; to say nothing of their hatred for the autonomy the province enjoys due to its Albanian majority. As the Milicija (Police, translatable as 'militia') were there a mostly federal institution, with Albanians but also other Republic members (the 'Združeni Odredi Milicije' - Combined Units of Police), they had little love for Serbian nationalism and responded harshly. Milošević, impromptu, declared famously "Niko ne sme da vas bije!" ('None may beat you!') - and from that moment on, a pupil had become a new man. An icon, for Serbia's grievances. A Ranković - without Ranković's Yugoslavism or determined communism.

Then on September 3rd, after much maneuvering, tragedy would become an opening. An Albanian JNA conscript, Aziz Kelmendi, opens fire on his sleeping comrades - killing four and injuring five. Despite the composition being multi-ethnic, the Serbian media, already largely overtaken by Slobo, runs a frenzy against the Albanians and causes near-riots. Alarmed by the hysteria, the party chief of Belgrade, Dragiša Pavlović, trying to calm tensions down - invited the media and declared that the issues could not be resolved with "Lightly promised speed". . .leading merely to his own media lynching. The true crux however, was that before a closed session of the Presidency of the Central Committee of Serbia, Stambolić went out on a limb and sent a letter of support to the Belgrade central committee, defending Pavlović. Slobo leaked this, declaring it factionalism, one of the worst charges in the Socialist system.

A meeting of the full central committee is called. Slobodan ensures that, with his allies in the media, the whole thing will be televised. And yet, one man will perhaps be more relevant, than either Slobodan or Ivan. For one man, despite Slobo's work in the SDB (security service), and Ivan's technocratic expertise, controls the SUBNOR (Yugoslavia's massive veterans organization) and JNA's massive shadow.

That man, is General Nikola Ljubičić.

- @generals_choice: And the General must make his choice.

@generals_choice
new-page: true
face-image: img/bu/scenes/1987_09_23_general.jpg

= What of Serbia's Party?

Ljubičić is not unknown to party intrigues, or Yugoslav ones. He has been described as Tito's right hand man, he was the defense secretary for the whole of Yugoslavia, the President of the Presidency of SR Serbia - and now he is a member of the Presidency of Yugoslavia. And yet, very rarely has the choice between Serbia's own figures been so unnecessarily muddied.

On one hand, he has already promised Slobo his support, and Slobo has made it perfectly clear that he will fight for a stronger, more centrally-held Yugoslavia. And that is, of course, great for Ljubičić. He has been forced to chafe with practically sovereign Republics that defy the center, and it is essentially the consensus of every single senior JNA officer that the country needs to be tightened a little. The issue however, at least in the long-term, is what then contains Milošević? If he helps kill Ivan, politically of course, then what's to stop Slobo from killing all of them? Slobodan has, after all, proved to be a ruthless expert of party politics. Then again, so has Nikola - who is to say Slobo will be anything more but a pawn for JNA's necessary centralizations?

On the other hand, Stambolić is not a bad man - but from Nikola's perspective, he very well may be a weakling. A technocrat, a somewhat centrist socialist within the Yugoslav framework, of course he too agrees with the need for centralization, both of SR Serbia (and against Kosovo's autonomy) and SFR Yugoslavia. However he is a milder man, determined to do it with consensus from the other Republics, and the leadership itself. Noble goals - but can Stambolić accomplish them? Moreover, is it worth changing horses mid-fight, and to support what is essentially a shallow center-mass? Nikola is unsure, but perhaps it may prove wise to clip Slobodan's wings before it is too late.

Perhaps however, one might need play a game of chess? Use the Rook of Pavlović, to take out Queen Slobo - after all, fighting the slow King Stambolić is easier than the lightning rod the general has seen the past four months. On the obverse, keeping a Queen on-side, might be of greater worth than a not-yet encircled King? So are, the General's choices. . .

